  "body": "### Edit:\r\n\r\nThis tries to make progress on https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1679\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nThings this PR attempts to accomplish:\r\n\r\n - Be a little more explicit in variable usage with the lists `results` and `items`.\r\n - Hopefully make the recursion a little better. I opted to call the recursive substep procedure right away on the element that `innerText` is called on instead of each of its children (which happens eventually anyways).\r\n - Moved the `If node has no associated CSS box` check to before we recurse. My rationale for this was that all child nodes of a `display: none` parent will always have `display: none`, and thus no CSS boxes. So it doesn't make sense to do a bunch of recursion on elements with no CSS boxes if we know what the result will be ahead of time. I guess this theoretically affects the performance of the algorithm by eliminating work, but I believe it is still a non-normative change (and hopefully a correct one?).\r\n\r\nOutstanding goals/questions:\r\n\r\n - How can we make the recursion even less worse? I know in [whatwg/console](https://github.com/whatwg/console) we use abstract algorithms that can actually be called with parameters and yield results. I feel like this algorithm could benefit from pulling the nested `<ol>` out into its own algorithm. Is this a common thing in most standards? I'd appreciate some info on how to do this (assuming there is agreement on its efficacy) and I could tack that onto this PR. This is mainly stemming from checkbox 5 in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1679#issue-171405454.\r\n - Substep 2 checks to see `If node has no associated CSS box`...could we make this `If node is not being rendered` and still be ok?",
